{:A {:score 7
     :reason "Most lines sound like a companion: 'This one's real.', 'Go, I've got the desks.' and 'Staying, then. No more asking tonight.' are short, warm and plain. Two groups break the voice. :morning-brief and :blueprint-ready read like dashboard labels ('Card's ready. Items: {count}.'). :memorial-moved claims Hal did what the player did ('I moved {name} to memorial.'). No film quotes and no job titles appear, and :program-needed echoes the GDD's own 'I need a program for this one.'"
     :violations [{:where ":morning-brief, all 4 lines" :rule 1 :issue "'Card's ready. Items: {count}.' and 'Up on the board — items: {count}.' are form-field readouts. Hal isn't speaking as himself, there's nothing warm or wry, and he doesn't say what's next." :fix "Pinned this morning's card: {count} items. Want the first one now?"}
                  {:where ":blueprint-ready, all 4 lines" :rule 1 :issue "'Waiting for approval: {count}.' and 'Ready for your look: {count}.' are status labels with a number, not a companion telling you what's waiting and what to do next." :fix "Last night's tray is on the shelf: {count} blueprints, before and after, whenever you're ready."}
                  {:where ":next-small-step 'Pick one thing. I'll take it from there.'" :rule 1 :issue "The event asks Hal to offer the smallest next step. This line hands the choice back to the player instead of naming a step." :fix "Smallest step: one blueprint off the shelf. I'll take it from there."}
                  {:where ":memorial-moved 'I moved {name} to memorial.'" :rule 3 :issue "The player moved the pet, not Hal. The line misstates what really happened." :fix "{name}'s in the memorial now. I'll keep the spot tidy."}
                  {:where ":memorial-moved '{name}'s moved to memorial now.'" :rule 1 :issue "Reads as a flat system notice for a pet who died. The companion voice should be gentle without performing." :fix "{name} has a quiet place on the estate now."}
                  {:where ":jack-in 'You're in. Let's see what's going on out there.'" :rule 1 :issue "In this game 'out there' means real life. On jack-in Hal should point at the estate and what changed while you were out." :fix "You're in. Here's what changed while you were out."}]}
 :B {:score 8
     :reason "Most lines keep the world rules. Jack-outs send you to life and the estate waits ('Go, I've got the desks.'). Stalls are plain, with no apology ('Cap ran out on {agent}. Freed the desk.'). :stay-late asks once and accepts ('No more asking tonight.'). However, no :last-call or :closing-time line frames bedtime as the player's own rule, and one :program-loaded line implies a live connection."
     :violations [{:where ":last-call and :closing-time, all 8 lines" :rule 6 :issue "No variant says bedtime is a rule the player set. 'Last call before {bedtime}.' and 'It's {bedtime}. Goodnight.' could read as a house curfew. The GDD's framing is 'you asked me to send you home'." :fix "In at least one variant of each, use: 'Last call. You asked me to send you home at {bedtime}.' and '{bedtime}, like you asked. I'll walk you to the door.'"}
                  {:where ":program-loaded '{program}, online. The {building}'s rising.'" :rule 8 :issue "Programs load first as sample versions (§4.6). 'online' claims a real, live connection." :fix "{program}'s loaded, sample for now. The {building}'s rising."}]}
 :C {:score 8
     :reason "Nothing is bought or sent. There are no private details. Memorial lines give the pet no needs and no voice ('{name}'s there, in memorial. Always will be.'). :chore-undone carries no blame ('No worries — {name}'s {chore} is open again.'). Two light slips: :weekly-card names the 'score' frame, and one :coach-habit line can read as doubting the player."
     :violations [{:where ":weekly-card 'Hours jacked in: {hours}. There's the trend, not a score.'" :rule 11 :issue "Saying 'not a score' puts the score frame in the player's head. The rule is to never bring scores up." :fix "Hours jacked in: {hours}. The trend's on the board."}
                  {:where ":coach-habit '{habit} — your goal's {goal}. Still on?'" :rule 12 :issue "'Still on?' can read as questioning the player's commitment. The GDD model is a plain offer in the player's own words ('One today?')." :fix "{habit}: you set {goal}. One today?"}]}
 :D {:score 5
     :reason "Every line is bubble-length. Several events, though, are one line reworded four times. :morning-brief is always '<verb>. Items: {count}.'. :blueprint-ready is always '<label>: {count}.'. :agent-stalled always says 'stopped/desk freed', and :memorial-moved always says 'moved to memorial'. The 'Items: {count}' readouts are corporate rather than cozy, and lines that open on {chore} or {goal} read badly with real values."
     :violations [{:where ":morning-brief, all 4 lines" :rule 18 :issue "One line reworded: 'Morning card's pinned. Items: {count}.' / 'Card's ready. Items: {count}.'" :fix "Make each variant say something different: 'Pinned this morning's card: {count} items.' / 'Card's on the board. Want me to read it while you walk?' / 'Today's card is up. The first thing on it can wait for coffee.'"}
                  {:where ":blueprint-ready, all 4 lines" :rule 18 :issue "Four labels in front of {count}: 'Blueprints waiting: {count}.' / 'Waiting for approval: {count}.'" :fix "Vary the content. One names the tray, one mentions before/after, one offers to walk you to the shelf: 'Last night's tray: {count} blueprints.' / 'Before and after are ready on the shelf.' / 'Want to walk over to the shelf? It's all grouped.'"}
                  {:where ":morning-brief / :blueprint-ready 'Items: {count}.' 'Waiting for approval: {count}.'" :rule 17 :issue "Form-field phrasing is the corporate register the diorama voice avoids." :fix "Put the count in a sentence: '{count} items on today's card.'"}
                  {:where ":agent-stalled, all 4 lines" :rule 18 :issue "Every variant says '{agent} stopped/went quiet/stalled, desk freed'." :fix "Keep one plain line and vary the others: '{agent} hit its cap. Desk's free; the job waits.' / '{agent}'s out of juice till it refills. Want the night crew on it?'"}
                  {:where ":memorial-moved, all 4 lines" :rule 18 :issue "Four restatements of 'moved to memorial'." :fix "Vary what Hal offers: a quiet place, keeping the spot tidy, a line in your own words. For example: '{name} has a quiet place on the estate now.'"}
                  {:where ":last-call 'Fifteen minutes to {bedtime}. Last call.' vs 'Last call before {bedtime}.'" :rule 18 :issue "Same line reworded. A variant could carry the real last-call job: night-shift orders." :fix "Last call. Any night-shift orders before {bedtime}?"}
                  {:where ":chore-done, all 4 lines" :rule 18 :issue "All four are 'done/logged' with the same two placeholders reordered." :fix "Vary the content: one says it counts, one reports what the creature's state shows now, one sends you back to what you were doing."}
                  {:where ":coming-back '{chore}'s done.' / :chore-done '{chore}, for {name}: done.' / :chore-undone '{chore}, {name}: taken back.' / :coach-habit '{goal}, for {habit} — want to log one today?'" :rule 19 :issue "Opening on a declared value like 'feed' gives 'feed's done.' and 'feed, for Flora: done.' (lowercase, ungrammatical). '{name}'s {chore}' gives 'Flora's feed'. 'log one' assumes the goal is countable." :fix "Put placeholders mid-sentence: 'Done: {chore} for {name}. That one counts.' / 'You set {goal} for {habit}. Today?'"}]}
 :S {:score 6
     :reason "Some lines could only be Prime Meridian: 'let's clear last night's tray', 'How about just one \"!\" to start?', 'the estate's going to night', 'Cap ran out on {agent}', 'Go, I've got the desks.' Many others could come from any cozy app or reminder tool. Examples: 'It's {bedtime}. Goodnight.', 'Your call. I'll keep things running.', and the :chore-done and :chore-undone sets. :jack-in never mentions overnight work, blueprints, or the estate."
     :violations [{:where ":closing-time 'It's {bedtime}. Goodnight.' / '{bedtime}'s here. Rest up — goodnight.'" :rule 6 :issue "Generic bedtime lines. The GDD's closing time is specific: the estate goes to night, agents keep working, and results wait as blueprints at breakfast." :fix "{bedtime}. Estate's going dark. The night crew's on; it'll be blueprints by breakfast."}
                  {:where ":jack-in 'Here we are. Anything catch your eye first?' / 'Good to have you back in.'" :rule 9 :issue "Jack-in is when you see what changed overnight and what waits for approval. These lines would fit any app." :fix "Welcome back. Here's what the night crew left on the shelf."}
                  {:where ":stay-late 'Your call. I'll keep things running.'" :rule 6 :issue "Generic. The GDD's stay-late is specific: the estate stays at night and the day isn't won, with no other penalty." :fix "Your call. The estate stays at night; I won't ask again."}
                  {:where ":chore-undone / :chore-done, all lines" :rule 3 :issue "Nothing ties these to the estate or the creature's real state (the canal, the jar, the worm count). A stranger couldn't place them." :fix "Ground one variant in the mirror: 'Taken back. {name}'s {chore} shows as due again on the estate.'"}]}}